WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.560
We just hit a million subscribers! I can&nbsp;
hardly believe that so many of you Fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.800
Scholars are enjoying the Papers! Thank you so&nbsp;
much for all the love! In a previous episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:24.400
we explored an absolutely insane idea. The idea&nbsp;
was to unleash a learning algorithm on a dataset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:31.040
that contains images and videos of cities,&nbsp;
then take a piece of video footage from a game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:37.680
and translate it into a real movie. It&nbsp;
is an absolute miracle that this works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:46.080
and it not only works, but it works reliably and&nbsp;
interactively. And it also works much better than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:52.240
its predecessors. Now, we discussed that the&nbsp;
input video game footage is pretty detailed.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:58.960
And I was wondering, what if we don’t create&nbsp;
the entire game in such detail. What about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.280
creating just the bare minimum,&nbsp;
a draft of the game if you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:09.280
and let the algorithm do the heavy lifting.&nbsp;
Let’s call this world to world translation!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:15.120
So, is world to world translation&nbsp;
possible, or is this science fiction?

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:21.520
Fortunately, scientists at NVIDIA and Cornell&nbsp;
University thought of that problem and came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:27.920
up with a remarkable solution. But, the first&nbsp;
question is - what form should this draft take?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.440
And they say, it should be a Minecraft world, or&nbsp;
in other words, a landscape assembled from little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:46.000
blocks. Yes, that is simple enough indeed. So this&nbsp;
goes in. And now, let’s see what comes out. Oh my!

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:53.920
It created water, it understands the concept of&nbsp;
an island, and it created a beautiful landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:02:01.840
also, with vegetation. Insanity.
It even seems to have some concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.320
of reflections, although they will need&nbsp;
some extra work to get perfectly right.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:13.840
But, what about artistic control? Do we get&nbsp;
this one solution, or can we give more detailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:21.280
instructions to the technique? Yes we can! Look at&nbsp;
that. Since the training data contains desert and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:29.760
snowy landscapes too, is also supports them as&nbsp;
outputs. Whoa, this is getting wild. I like it.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:36.080
And it even supports interpolation, which&nbsp;
means that we can create one landscape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.920
and ask the AI to create a&nbsp;
blend between different styles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:46.480
We just look at the output animations, and pick&nbsp;
the one that we like best. Absolutely amazing.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.280
What I also really liked is that&nbsp;
it also supports rendering fog.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.720
But this is not some trivial fog technique,&nbsp;
no-no, look how beautifully it occludes the trees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:06.560
If we look under the hood, oh my! I am a&nbsp;
light transport researcher by trade, and boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.520
am I happy to see the authors having done&nbsp;
their homework. Look, we are not playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:18.080
games here, the technique contains bona-fide&nbsp;
volumetric light transmission calculations.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.720
Now, this is not the first technique to perform&nbsp;
this kind of world to world translation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.560
What about the competition? As you see, there&nbsp;
are many prior techniques here, but there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:36.240
one key issue that almost all of them share.&nbsp;
So, what is that? Oh yes, much like with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:42.080
other video game papers, the issue is the lack of&nbsp;
temporal coherence, which means that the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:49.040
techniques don’t remember they it did a few images&nbsp;
earlier, and may create a drastically different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:55.680
series of images. And the result is this kind of&nbsp;
flickering that is often a deal-breaker regardless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:02.000
of how good the technique is otherwise. Look,&nbsp;
the new method does this significantly better.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:09.040
This could help level generation for computer&nbsp;
games, creating all kinds simulations, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:15.920
if it improves some more, these could maybe even&nbsp;
become backdrops to be used in animated movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.440
Now, of course, this is still not perfect,&nbsp;
some of the outputs are still blocky.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:28.880
But, with this method, creating virtual worlds&nbsp;
has never been easier. I cannot believe that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.960
can have a learning-based algorithm where the&nbsp;
input is one draft world, and it transforms it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:42.640
to a much more detailed and beautiful one. Yes,&nbsp;
it has its limitations, but just imagine what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:48.240
we will be able to do two more papers down the&nbsp;
line. Especially given that the quality of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:54.560
results can be algorithmically measured, which is&nbsp;
a godsend for comparing this to future methods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:00.720
And for now, huge congratulations to NVIDIA&nbsp;
and Cornell University for this amazing paper.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.520
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

